# `math::Function::cosh`

[Back to `math::Function`](../Function.md)

The hyperbolic cosine of a function, as a new function.

## Signature

```cpp
math::Function math::Function::cosh() const;
```

## What It Does

`f.cosh()` builds `x ↦ cosh f(x) = (e^{f} + e^{−f}) / 2`. It is defined for every argument, is even, has its minimum value 1 at 0, and grows exponentially for large `|x|`. Its derivative is `(cosh u)′ = u′ sinh u`.

The graph of `cosh` is the catenary, the shape of a hanging chain. With [`sinh`](sinh.md) it satisfies `cosh² − sinh² = 1`.

## Returns

A new `Function` whose value is `cosh` of the receiver's value, with the same variables and output shape.

## Errors

- The function must be defined.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

`cosh` evaluated on a small grid, next to the standard-library value. Each element is computed independently, so the result has the shape of the input.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.cosh(); });
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-2.0, 0.0, 1.0, 3.0});
  out.writeln("x:           ", x.items<double>());
  out.writeln("f(x):        ", f({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("std::cosh(x):", pluss::make_series({std::cosh((-2.0)), std::cosh((0.0)), std::cosh((1.0)), std::cosh((3.0))}).items<double>());
}
```

The two rows agree to the printed digits.

### Example 2

The derivative rule. Differentiating `cosh` with `operators::differential` gives the exact formula, `(cosh x)′ = sinh x`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.cosh(); });
  const math::Function df = operators::differential("x")(f);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-2.0, 0.0, 1.0, 3.0});
  const auto exact = [](double v) { return std::sinh(v); };
  out.writeln("f'(x):        ", df({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("closed form:  ", pluss::make_series({exact(-2.0), exact(0.0), exact(1.0), exact(3.0)}).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

The identity `cosh² x − sinh² x = 1`, built in one callable and evaluated at three points.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const math::Function identity = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.cosh() * x.cosh() - x.sinh() * x.sinh(); });
  out.writeln("cosh^2 - sinh^2:", identity({pluss::make_series({-2.0, 0.5, 3.0})}).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4

The catenary `y = a cosh(x/a)` has curvature radius `a cosh²(x/a)`; here the minimum height `a` is the value at 0, and the slope there is zero.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const double a = 2.0;
  const math::Function chain = math::function({"x"}, [&](math::Function x) { return a * (x / a).cosh(); });
  const math::Function slope = operators::differential("x")(chain);
  out.writeln("height at 0:", chain(0.0).item<double>(), "  slope at 0:", slope(0.0).item<double>(), "  slope at 2:", slope(2.0).item<double>());
  out.writeln("sinh(1)    =", std::sinh(1.0));
}
```

